  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#916;H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#916;E</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>&#916;n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub><mi>RT</mi></math></p><p>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub></math> (for the given reaction) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#916;H</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>&#916;E</mi></math></p><p>In exothermic reactions, heat from the system is lost to the surrounding. For such reactions, ∆H is negative.</p><p>In endothermic reactions, heat is absorbed by the system from the surroundings. For such reactions, ∆H is positive.</p>",
